welcome to the daily climate show stay  with us coming up how will germany keep  warm in winter after russia slashes  crucial gas supplies  floods heat waves and wildfires extreme  weather across the u.s continues as the  country feels the impact of climate  change  [Music]  and why cats have been declared an  invasive species in poland  [Music]  welcome to the daily climate show where  we track the changes happening to our  world hear from those affected and  report on the race for solutions now  energy prices in the uk could reach  nearly 4  000 pounds a year after russia curbed  gas supplies to europe the warning from  utilities consultancy bfy comes as eu  countries move to reduce their reliance  on russian gas so after gazprom halved  gas supplies to europe in germany  in germany russia has been cutting flows  to the country with it now operating at  less than a fifth of its normal capacity  the move which russia says is due to  pipeline maintenance is being seen as  further escalation of an energy standoff  between moscow and the european union  that will make it harder and costlier  for the bloc to fill up storage ahead of  the winter heating season  well we can talk now to our europe  correspondent adam parsons who is in  cologne adam thanks for joining us it's  a really tricky time for germany isn't  it because  not least because they are switching off  their nuclear power plants by the end of  the year  exactly right they have found themselves  in something of a of a bind here you  have to go back over quite a few years  of german energy policy and it's not  pretty reading and a yep they they  absolutely said angela merkel said we  are getting rid of nuclear power after  the fukushima uh disaster and they're  pressing on with that  trouble is they've also been very  reliant on russian gas well that turned  out to be a bad idea remember they built  nordstrom 2 that pipeline that was never  opened  and of course they are still using a lot  of coal not just any old coal but  lignite really dirty uh grubby cult so  that's the energy mix they find  themselves in at the moment uh the other  bit of that was nordstrom the original  nordstrom one pipeline that connects  germany to russia and that is the one  that gazprom is now dialing down there  are those who think that eventually  gazprom might switch that one off  entirely and germany is in a bit of a  panic because it relies on that russian  gas yes he's trying to find other ways  of filling the gap of investing in  renewable energy and trying to get oil  and gas from other countries but the  bottom line is it hasn't plugged the gap  yes so it is nervously watching that  north stream pipeline and seeing uh  about the flows but at the same time  it's trying to find ways of reducing the  use  of energy and that's what brings us to  places like uh cologne where a raft of  measures are being brought in street  lamps are being dimmed at night for  instance swimming pools are not being  heated in the way that they normally  would be we've been out to see a housing  estate where now the thermometer is  being turned down the heating is not  going to rise as high as it had done  previously now clearly if you live in a  place like that a you might be annoyed  at the heating and b we spoke to plenty  people who say they're worried about  uncertainty about what comes next but  what is likely is higher bills in  germany as anywhere else lots of  pressure  on the government to do something about  it maybe as in britain pressure to strip  away some of those green levels now  remember the green party here is part of  the coalition today we had confirmation  that 25 billion euros was going to be  put into a climate fund they have talked  a good game about embracing  environmentalism but they did that  before this huge looming energy crisis  adam parsons in cologne in germany for  us thanks very much for that  now environmental campaigners say much  more time needs to be given to the  climate crisis in the tory leadership  contest organizations including the  world wildlife fund greenpeace and the  green alliance are calling for the  candidates to be better tested on their  climate credentials it comes as business  secretary quasi qatang who's backing  this trust says climate issues are being  taken seriously after the u.s climate  envoy john kerry warned the leadership  hopefuls not to [ __ ] with net zero  plans by slashing green levees  we've got energy prices that have gone  through the roof they're global it's a  global issue  uh gas prices have gone through the roof  we've got russia invading ukraine we've  got russia threatening to turn off the  gas supply to large parts of western  europe and in that context this trust is  saying we've got to look at the energy  mix we've got to look at the costs  and we may have to suspend green levees  as a consequence because this is an  extraordinary time  let's bring in our political  correspondent ali fortescue ali what do  we know about the tory hopefuls climate  credential so far what's been discussed  well it is right that their climate  credentials have not been front and  center of this campaign so far what has  been front and center is the economy  that's been the big dividing line there  have over the last couple of days in the  tv debates we've had been a couple of  questions about climate change rishi  sunak has been talking about things like  technology the need to insulate homes  there's trust has been talking about  food waste but really i think there is  pressure  on them for there to be more more  commitments and more clarity on how they  want to tackle climate change what we do  know is that they are both talking about  the cost of living crisis on that liz  trust says that she wants to suspend  green levees you heard her back at quasi  quatern talking about it there on energy  bills that though is obviously we know  green levees is all about helping the  transition toward clean energy so there  have been criticisms that she is not  someone that is committed to net zero  she says she is but we need to find  different ways of getting there but at  the moment as i say i think there is  definitely pressure on to her to show  how exactly she's going to be doing that  rishi sunak he um overnight has been  talking about cutting vat on energy  bills it's been criticized as a u-turn  because he previously  was criticizing labor when they  suggested a similar policy but today the  ifs a warning that maybe that will have  an impact too because could that drive  up energy use so there are questions  when it comes to their climate  commitments and what they are saying  about how they plan to solve the big  issue of the cost of living crisis um  but they do say that they do have  commitments and that they are going to  be talking about it more but i think all  eyes will be on the next few debates and  the hustings to see whether they really  can deliver something on climate change  we will watch with interest ally  fortitude in westminster for us thanks  for that  now let's get a bit more of today's  climate news the isla isle of man has  become the first place in the uk to see  a hosepipe ban enforced the restrictions  come into force at midnight on friday  and after an unusually dry spring which  was followed by a 50 drop in average  rainfall in june anyone caught flouting  the hosepipe ban could be fined up to  2000 pounds  japan's industry minister koichi  hagayuda has been made the country's  first ever green transformation minister  the new position has been created as  part of the government's plans to  achieve carbon neutrality by 2050  through changes to its economic and  energy structures it comes as japan's  administration holds its first meeting  today planning green transformation  policies  domestic cats have been labeled an  invasive alien species by a polish  scientific institute it follows a  growing scientific consensus that  felines damage birds and wildlife  because of the numbers they hunt and  kill domestic cats were added to the  database already containing more than  1700 species  now with destructive fires flooding and  intense heat so far it's been a week of  extreme weather in the u.s  at least one person has died after  record-breaking rain caused flash  flooding in the city of saint louis the  body was found covered in more than  eight feet of water  dozens were rescued after 30 centimeters  of rain destroyed homes and submerged  cars in the area  more than 36 million people across the  u.s are living under extreme heat  advisories watches or warnings parts of  oregon and washington state have  excessive heat warnings with  temperatures expected to reach 37  degrees in the city of portland where  the state's mayor has declared a state  of emergency  more than 500 wildfires are burning in  the northern state of alaska millions of  acres have burned there after recent  heat waves droughts and lightning  strikes started blazes  firefighters are continuing to battle a  huge wildfire in mariposa county near  yosemite national park in california  more than 2 000 firefighters have been  at the site since it started on friday  around 6 000 residents have been  evacuated sky's joe pike sent the latest  from there  it is the complete lack of color that is  most striking here it's a grey landscape  the trees have been stripped bare of  leaves and it's almost silent  there is no bird song  we pretty regularly though hear the  sounds of helicopters flying overhead  dropping retardant  over the flames there are many different  factors that contribute to wildfires  including the intense drought in this  state low humidity high heat dry parched  vegetation but if you speak to  firefighters who've been dealing with  these sorts of emergencies over decades  they say they've sensed a shift  we are having  rapid growth of fires extreme fire  behavior that we normally don't see  until later in the fire season in  september and october we expect  most fires to grow very rapidly  and with that it will also deplete  resources so we'll be  we'll be shorter on resources for some  fires and we we definitely expect to be  busy  california's wildfire season will  continue into the summer and through to  the autumn it's likely to get more  intense as time passes and the resources  of the fire services then could really  be  under pressure of course the residents  who call this area home who have  family and friends here face a difficult  choice especially those whose homes have  been destroyed do they try and leave and  start a new life elsewhere or do they  try and rebuild knowing that all of this  could happen again  kew gardens has secured a multi-million  pound investment to help fund its  research into climate change and how to  solve the global food crisis the funding  from greensphere capital will help pay  for extra researchers who will look into  climate change resistant crops  zero carbon fertilizer and plant and  cell-based meat products  well as it happens we'll be visiting kew  gardens later in the week where tom heap  will be taking a look at how we need to  adapt our gardening habits to the  changing climate and tom can put your  questions to an expert just stand the qr  code on your screen right now to submit  a question and we'll do our best to give  you an answer on the climate show with  tom heap on saturday and sunday at 3 30  and 7 30 p.m that is all from us today  thanks very much for watching we'll see  you soon

